yle":{"borderLeft":"4px solid #22d3ee","paddingLeft":"24px","margin":"24px 0","fontStyle":"italic","color":"#a3a3a3"},"children":[["$","p","0",{"style":{"margin":"8px 0"},"children":[["$","strong","0",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"Source:"}],["$","span","1",{"children":" Company filings, Bloomberg, Financial Times, analyst estimates"}]]}]]}],["$","p","71",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The table reveals something important: "}],["$","strong","1",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"ByteDance is committing a higher share of its revenue to AI infrastructure than any Chinese peer, and it's approaching the investment intensity of US hyperscalers."}],["$","span","2",{"children":" While Alibaba and Tencent are spending more conservatively (as percentages of revenue), ByteDance is betting the company on AI in a way that mirrors Meta's 82% capex-to-revenue ratio."}]]}],["$","p","73",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"That bet makes strategic sense when you consider what ByteDance is protecting. Its core business — short-form video recommendation — is itself an AI product. Every TikTok and Douyin feed is powered by recommendation algorithms that require massive inference capacity. If ByteDance loses the AI infrastructure race, it doesn't just lose a new product line. It loses the engine that powers its entire business."}]]}],["$","hr","75",{"style":{"border":"none","borderTop":"1px solid #1a1a1a","margin":"32px 0"}}],["$","h2","77",{"style":{"fontSize":"28px","fontWeight":"600","margin":"40px 0 20px","color":"#f5f5f5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Inside the $29 Billion: Where the Money Goes"}]]}],["$","p","79",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"ByteDance's 200 billion yuan isn't sitting in a bank account waiting to be spent. It's already flowing through a complex supply chain that spans six continents and involves some of the most strategically sensitive technology on Earth."}]]}],["$","h3","81",{"style":{"fontSize":"22px","fontWeight":"600","margin":"32px 0 16px","color":"#f5f5f5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The NVIDIA Problem"}]]}],["$","p","83",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The single largest line item in ByteDance's AI budget is NVIDIA accelerators. According to sources cited by the Financial Times, the company planned to allocate roughly "}],["$","strong","1",{"style":{"color":"#f5f5f5","fontWeight":"700"},"children":"85 billion yuan ($12.5 billion)"}],["$","span","2",{"children":" specifically to AI processors in its original 160 billion yuan budget. That's more than the entire AI infrastructure spending of most countries."}]]}],["$","p","85",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The problem is that NVIDIA can't sell ByteDance its best chips. US export controls, tightened in 2024 and 2025, restrict access to the H100, H200, and the newer Blackwell series. What ByteDance can buy — the H20, a deliberately downgraded version of the H100 — still costs roughly $20,000 per chip but delivers significantly less performance than the US-market versions."}]]}],["$","p","87",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"ByteDance's response has been threefold: buy everything it can legally acquire, lease overseas capacity where export controls don't apply, and develop alternatives."}]]}],["$","div","96",{"style":{"overflowX":"auto","margin":"24px 0"},"children":["$","table",null,{"style":{"width":"100%","borderCollapse":"collapse","fontSi