 for AI workloads, offers both capacity and a geopolitical hedge — South Korea is perceived as a more neutral ground than Taiwan for Chinese companies seeking to diversify supply chains."}]]}],["$","p","105",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"If SeedChip succeeds, ByteDance would join a small club of companies — Google, Amazon, Meta, and Microsoft — that have designed their own AI accelerators at scale. If it fails, the company will remain dependent on NVIDIA and Huawei for the foreseeable future."}]]}],["$","hr","107",{"style":{"border":"none","borderTop":"1px solid #1a1a1a","margin":"32px 0"}}],["$","h2","109",{"style":{"fontSize":"28px","fontWeight":"600","margin":"40px 0 20px","color":"#f5f5f5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"Why This Matters: The Global Ripple Effects"}]]}],["$","p","111",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"ByteDance's $29 billion investment isn't just a Chinese story. It has tangible consequences for the global tech economy in at least three ways."}]]}],["$","h3","113",{"style":{"fontSize":"22px","fontWeight":"600","margin":"32px 0 16px","color":"#f5f5f5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"1. The Memory Crunch"}]]}],["$","p","115",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"AI chips are useless without memory, and memory is becoming the second bottleneck in global AI infrastructure. ByteDance's massive HBM orders are competing with orders from Amazon, Google, Meta, and Microsoft for a finite supply of SK Hynix, Samsung, and Micron output."}]]}],["$","p","117",{"style":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.8","margin":"16px 0","color":"#e5e5e5"},"children":[["$","span","0",{"children":"The result is a seller's market for memory. HBM3 prices have risen 30-50% since early 2025,